Yes, the core idea is sound, but I would broaden it from **“everything runs through the index”** to:

> **TemplarOS should prohibit polling for local state whenever an authoritative event source exists. Services should normally sleep or not exist at all until the state they depend on changes.**

The file index then becomes one major **state authority**, not the authority for the whole machine.

The reason for that distinction is that your basic observation is almost right, but “disk or RAM changed” is too low-level to be useful. A service might care about:

* a file appearing;
* a USB device connecting;
* Wi-Fi losing its link;
* memory pressure crossing a threshold;
* a process exiting;
* the battery reaching 10%;
* a new display connecting;
* a network packet arriving;
* a D-Bus property changing;
* a remote server changing;
* a certificate becoming old;
* 6 PM arriving.

They all ultimately involve memory somewhere, but monitoring RAM writes would be vastly more expensive than polling. Linux already exposes higher-level event interfaces for most of them.

## The architecture I would use

Think of TemplarOS as having **event authorities**:

```text
                 TemplarOS state changes
                         │
       ┌─────────────────┼──────────────────┐
       │                 │                  │
 filesystem         hardware/device      resources
       │                 │                  │
 unified index         udev             PSI/cgroups
       │                 │                  │
       └─────────────┬───┴──────────────────┘
                     │
              kernel/network
                     │
                  netlink
                     │
              applications
                     │
                D-Bus/socket
                     │
             time itself changes
                     │
              systemd timers
```

Services subscribe to the **highest-level authoritative source** appropriate to them.

Linux is already built to support this kind of design. Netlink provides asynchronous kernel notifications, fanotify provides filesystem event notification, and PSI can literally be configured so a process blocks in `poll()` until CPU/memory/I/O pressure crosses a chosen threshold. ([man7.org][1])

That's much better than:

```text
while true {
    inspect_world();
    sleep(5 seconds);
}
```

---

# The file index can eliminate a lot of polling

This part of your idea is particularly strong.

Suppose something currently does:

```text
every 10 seconds:
    scan ~/Downloads
    determine whether anything appeared
```

That should absolutely disappear.

The unified index already knows:

```text
generation 8291:
    CREATE ~/Downloads/foo.pdf
```

So the service can express:

```text
I care about:
    path under ~/Downloads
    type = file
    maybe extension = pdf
```

and remain asleep otherwise.

Or something checks whether a Git repository gained/removed files.

Same answer.

Or Silvaview repeatedly scans directories looking for storage changes.

Again, don't do that.

The index already maintains the namespace and relevant metadata.

This gives you a useful rule:

> **No Templar component may independently rescan a filesystem namespace already represented by the shared index unless it is repairing/verifying that index.**

That is worth making an architectural invariant.

---

# But don't have every service literally query SQLite

That's how the idea could go wrong.

You don't want:

```text
                    index.sqlite
                   /     |      \
                  /      |       \
           resourced   shell    updater
              |          |         |
             ...        ...       ...
```

with random processes treating the index database as shared memory.

Instead:

```text
filesystem
    ↓
lege-filed / index owner
    ↓
persistent indexed state
    ↓
typed API
    ├── Search(...)
    ├── ChangesSince(generation)
    ├── Subscribe(...)
    └── GetState(...)
```

Only the index owner touches its database.

Consumers depend on its **API and generations**, not its SQLite representation.

That lets you change SQLite, FTS, schemas, or even the entire backing implementation later.

---

# There is an even stronger idea hiding here: state + events

A raw event stream isn't enough.

Consider:

```text
service sleeps
file changes
event emitted
service launches later
```

If it depended purely on seeing the event live, it missed it.

Your index architecture already solves this elegantly through generations:

```text
Service last handled generation: 8300

Current generation:              8341

ChangesSince(8300)
        ↓
41 changes
```

That means a service doesn't need to remain running merely to avoid missing something.

This is extremely valuable.

I'd make this a general Templar principle:

> **Whenever practical, event sources should have recoverable state or sequence numbers so consumers do not have to remain resident simply to observe events.**

For files, that's your indexed generations.

For other subsystems, the domain owner often already maintains the current state.

So a service can do:

```text
wake
↓
ask current state/change sequence
↓
act
↓
exit
```

rather than live forever.
